{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"IMPLEMENTATION NOTE 2026-08-11 — one plan premise was wrong; the guard is sound for a better reason, and is stricter than the plan authorized.\n\nThe plan justified restricting the short-circuit to 1-, 2-, and 4-quad hex forms by asserting those 'cannot collide with an alias' because wordlist aliases are adj-noun / adj-noun-noun. That premise is FALSE: 'dead' is in ticket-wordlist-v2.txt, and both 'dead' and 'deed' are in ticket-wordlist.txt, so 'dead-deed' is a generatable two-word alias that also has hex-quad shape. The plan-review E4 advisory flagged exactly this and was right.\n\nThe guard is sound anyway, via CONTROL FLOW in _ids.resolve_ticket_id rather than wordlist content:\n- 4-quad matches _FULL_ID_RE and returns the exact-name lookup before the alias scan;\n- 2-quad matches _SHORT_ID_RE, whose branch ends in an unconditional 'return None' before the alias scan, so even a real hex-shaped stored alias does not resolve by that name;\n- 1-quad cannot be an alias at all (aliases are two or three words, or an 8-char no-dash fallback), and the implementation additionally never skips a ref equal to the target's own effective alias, covering a pathological stored alias;\n- 3-quad forms DO reach the alias scan and are excluded from the short-circuit entirely.\n\nThe alias-collision test therefore asserts the real invariant instead of the plan's wording: it builds a tracker whose OTHER ticket has stored alias 'dead-deed' and asserts resolve_ticket_id('dead-deed', tracker) is None, i.e. skipping it changes no outcome. The implementation skips a strict subset of what the plan authorized, so no acceptance criterion is weakened.\n\nDeliberately NOT done: the 'bound the history walk' idea (a git log --max-count cap). With the short-circuit the walk is O(commits) string comparisons and zero filesystem work, so a cap buys nothing measurable, and it WOULD change behavior — capping the log silently stops finding the fixing commit for older bugs, conflicting with the constraint that this change alter no resolution outcomes. The existing 300s _GIT_TIMEOUT watchdog still covers a wedged filesystem.\n\nVerified RED before GREEN: against the pre-fix blame.py the new tests fail 10/15, including the two noise tests failing with the exact reported text 'Error: Ambiguous prefix ...'; the 5 behaviour-preservation tests pass against BOTH versions, which is the negative control proving resolution semantics are unchanged.\n\nFollow-up worth its own ticket (NOT fixed here): _engine_support/lookups.py:47 GENERATES display ids as the 'shortest unambiguous dash-stripped prefix (min 4 chars)', so rebar still PRINTS the very 4-digit form these docs now deprecate. Changing that is a user-visible behavior change and was out of scope."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786506913621763001,"uuid":"8b45c7e1-15e0-4600-bf90-db6b7d469e1c"}